%erg 0.1
Title: The volume rig's pinned data directory silently does not take
Created: 2026-09-13
Author: claude

--- log ---
2026-09-13T10:47Z claude created

--- body ---
## Context

Arm 6 (2026-09-13, padme, the first volume run with a real main window) was
launched with `--data-dir /home/haduong/data/arm6/data`. The driver logged

    driver data directory: /home/haduong/data/arm6/data (pinned, not Zotero's default)

and the running Zotero reported, in the rig's OWN liveness read one minute later,

    {'ok': True, 'dataDir': '/home/haduong/Zotero', 'mainWindows': 1, ...}

`/home/haduong/data/arm6/data` was still empty at the end of the run. The
114-attachment Menagerie was imported into `/home/haduong/Zotero` instead, which
is Zotero's DEFAULT data directory, and the sitter extracted against it: that
directory now holds a 222-item library, `sdt-sitter-cache.jsonl` and
`sdt-sitter-last-shutdown.json`, all written during the run.

No damage occurred, and the reason is luck rather than a guard. The author's
real library lives at `/home/haduong/data/Zotero-fresh` (89 MB, untouched since
2026-09-09), so the default path happened to be empty. Had it been where most
installations put it, this run would have imported a fixture corpus into it and
run extraction over it.

This is the hazard ticket 0727 already recorded on 2026-09-12 — "the PROFILE was
throwaway; the DATA DIRECTORY never was" — and `--data-dir` is the option that
was added to close it. The option does not close it.

## The defect, stated as an observation

The pin is written into the profile's prefs and never read back. Zotero opened a
different data directory than the one requested, and nothing in the run compared
the two. The mechanism by which the pin fails to take is NOT established here:
candidates include the pref name, the moment it is written relative to first
launch, and a first-run data-directory prompt answering itself. Establish it
before fixing it.

The general form is the one this repository keeps meeting: the driver's log line
is a claim about an intention, and it reads exactly like a measurement. The
actual measurement was already being taken, one minute later, by the liveness
read — and no code compares them.

## Relevant files

- `bench/sitter_volume_experiment.py` — `--data-dir` (argparse ~:637), the
  "pinned, not Zotero's default" log line, and `liveness_code()` (~:385), which
  already returns `dataDir` and is where the contradiction was visible
- `tickets/0727-the-sitter-uninstalls-itself-update-url.erg` — the 2026-09-12
  entry that motivated `--data-dir`
- `/home/haduong/data/arm6/run-watch.txt` — the run this was found in

## Test

Red step first, and it is cheap: run the rig with `--data-dir` pointed at a
fresh directory and assert the liveness read's `dataDir` equals it. Against
today's code that assertion FAILS. It must fail before the fix lands, or the
fix is not shown to do anything.

## Actions

1. Establish why the pin does not take. Do not skip to a fix.
2. Make the run REFUSE, before importing anything, when the data directory
   Zotero actually opened is not the one requested. Refusing is the correct
   response rather than a warning: an import into the wrong library cannot be
   taken back, and the rig's whole purpose is to run unattended.
3. Replace the "pinned, not Zotero's default" log line with one that reports the
   directory actually in use, read from the host.

## Invariants

- The refusal is on the ACTUAL directory, never on the intended one.
- Do not weaken the existing guard that refuses a data directory already holding
  a `zotero.sqlite`; it is orthogonal and it did its job.

## Exit criteria

The rig cannot import into a data directory other than the one named on the
command line, a deliberate mismatch has been shown to stop a run, and the
mechanism behind the original failure is written down rather than worked around.
